# Serialization and Logging

## Logging costs more than you think

Every `Debug.Log` allocates (message string, stack trace capture) and stalls — in WebGL it also marshals to the browser console. Logs are for **events**, never for **frames**.

- **No logging in `Update`/`FixedUpdate`/`LateUpdate`** or per-frame coroutine bodies. Not even "temporarily".
- **Verbose/diagnostic logging** goes behind conditional compilation so it fully disappears from release builds — including the string interpolation that builds the message:

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — string is built and allocated even if filtered out
Debug.Log($"[Sim] tick {frame} units={units.Count}");

// ✅ GOOD — compiled out entirely in release
[System.Diagnostics.Conditional("ENABLE_VERBOSE_LOGS")]
private static void LogVerbose(string message)
{
    Debug.Log(message);
}
```

Note: `Conditional` removes the call, including argument evaluation — the interpolated string is never built when the symbol is off.

- Error/warning logs on genuine failure paths follow the fail-fast rule: keep them, with `[ClassName] Method — reason` context.

## Config: ScriptableObjects, not runtime JSON

Game configuration (tuning values, tables, curves) lives in **ScriptableObject assets**, deserialized by Unity at load — not JSON files parsed at runtime.

- SO fields are Inspector-editable, diff-able, and cost zero parse time.
- Runtime JSON parsing (`JsonUtility.FromJson`, Newtonsoft) is reserved for **wire data** (server payloads) — never for local static config.
- Keep a reference to the SO and read fields live; do not copy SO fields into private primitives at init (Inspector edits during play would stop applying).

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — parses a Resources JSON at startup
var cfg = JsonUtility.FromJson<TuningConfig>(File.ReadAllText(path));

// ✅ GOOD — SO asset, wired or loaded by address
[SerializeField] private TuningConfig _config;
public float MoveSpeed => _config.moveSpeed;
```

## Wire serialization

- Declare explicit `[JsonProperty("field_name")]` contracts on DTOs; do not rely on reflection-based name conventions (stripping hazard, see no-reflection rule).
- Deserialize once per message; never re-parse the same payload in multiple handlers.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `Debug.Log` in per-frame code; verbose logs behind `[Conditional]`
- [ ] Local config is ScriptableObject assets, not runtime-parsed JSON
- [ ] SO configs read live via reference, not copied to primitives
- [ ] DTOs use explicit `[JsonProperty]` attributes
